Information Systems, Cybersecurity & System Design – Sample Projects

This repository contains selected academic projects that demonstrate my background in information systems analysis, cybersecurity, data protection, system design, and user-centered technology development. These projects were completed at different stages of my academic development and show the progression of my experience from analyzing system requirements and designing interfaces to incorporating cybersecurity and data-protection principles into technical solutions.

Cybersecurity Training – Simulated DoD Scenario

This academic project involved developing a cybersecurity training presentation based on a simulated Department of Defense (DoD) management scenario. The project was not completed for or on behalf of the Department of Defense. It explored cybersecurity concepts including risk assessment and mitigation, access control, cryptography, firewalls, intrusion detection and prevention, security planning, and cybersecurity awareness.

The project demonstrates my ability to research technical cybersecurity concepts and translate them into structured educational material that can be understood by non-specialist users.

Secure Healthcare Information System – Use Case Design

This academic systems-analysis project involved developing detailed use cases for a simulated healthcare information system. The system included patient account management, appointment scheduling, authentication, and payment-processing workflows.

Security and privacy were incorporated into the system requirements through multi-factor authentication, unique user identification, access controls, encryption of personal information, and protection of sensitive payment data. For example, the login workflow required assigned user IDs and MFA, while system requirements specified encryption and restricted access to sensitive information.

This project demonstrates my ability to translate organizational requirements into structured system processes while considering cybersecurity, privacy, and appropriate access to sensitive information.

Low-Fidelity Application Prototype

This project demonstrates my experience with early-stage user-interface and system design. The low-fidelity mobile prototype presents a student-oriented application containing account sign-in and registration, events and groups, a buddy-matching system, tasks and points, and user profile/settings functionality.

The prototype also explores how users could connect with others based on factors such as location, courses, interests, likes, dislikes, and organizational involvement. This work demonstrates the process of translating proposed system functionality into a visual interface before full application development.

Connection to My Current Work

Together, these projects demonstrate a technical foundation spanning systems analysis, cybersecurity, data protection, requirements development, technical communication, and user-centered design. They also show the progression of my interest in designing technology that is both practical and security-conscious.

These skills now support my work involving cybersecurity readiness in educational environments, where I am applying systems analysis, data-informed decision-making, cybersecurity principles, and educational resource development to help make cybersecurity practices more accessible and actionable for K-12 users.

Note: These materials are provided as samples of prior academic work. Projects involving government, healthcare, or other organizational settings were based on academic or simulated scenarios unless otherwise stated.

